    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Cape in 1795 to guard the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and kept it permanently from 1806.</li><li>Frontier wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a>, the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1834 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a> shaped the colony and the whole region.</li><li>Self-governing from 1872 and enriched by diamonds, it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a> of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1910.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1853 the Cape franchise was based on property or income, not race, so some Black and Coloured men could vote; that right was removed in 1936 and 1956.</p></aside>
    </section>

