    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-elphinstone-1st-viscount-keith"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-elphinstone-1st-viscount-keith.jpg" alt="George Elphinstone, 1st Viscount Keith" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-elphinstone-1st-viscount-keith">George Elphinstone, 1st Viscount Keith</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vice-admiral_Lord_Keith,_Commander_in_Chief,_Mediterranean,_c_1799_-_John_Hoppner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Hoppner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> was built on the settlement founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC) in 1652. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> had become the French-allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sent a fleet under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-elphinstone-1st-viscount-keith">George Elphinstone</a> and troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-henry-craig">James Craig</a> to keep the sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> out of French hands. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muizenberg">Battle of Muizenberg</a> the company’s garrison surrendered in September 1795. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Peace of Amiens</a> returned the Cape to the Batavian Republic in 1803, but a British force under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-baird">David Baird</a> landed again and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a> on 8 January 1806, and the Netherlands formally ceded the colony in the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> convention of 1814. Britain inherited a colony of some 25,000 settlers of Dutch, German and French Huguenot descent, a larger population of enslaved people brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> labourers, many of them dispossessed of their land. Its eastern border lay roughly along the Fish River, where settler farmers competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> chiefdoms for grazing land. At first the Cape was valued above all as a naval station; only gradually did Britain take responsibility for its interior and frontier.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early nineteenth century the Cape economy rested on wine and wheat farming in the western districts, worked largely by slaves, and on livestock farming by trekboers in the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in 1807, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinance-50">Ordinance 50</a> of 1828 freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> from pass laws and gave them legal equality with settlers, a reform associated with the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-philip-missionary">John Philip</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished on 1 December 1834, followed by an apprenticeship that ended in 1838; compensation was paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and was widely felt by slave owners to be inadequate. Some 4,000 British immigrants, the 1820 Settlers, were placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-new-york">Albany</a> district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, and English replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as the language of government and courts in the 1820s. From the 1830s merino wool from the Eastern Cape became the leading export, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. The discovery of diamonds near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> in 1867 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> in 1871 transformed the economy, financing railways northwards and drawing migrant labour from across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> grew into a cosmopolitan port with Muslim, Christian and Jewish communities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-cape-town">South African College</a>, founded in 1829, became the University of Cape Town. By the 1904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the colony had about 2.4 million inhabitants.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-boer-war.jpg" alt="Second Boer War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Boer_War_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The British Army and The Illustrated London News, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history the colony’s main wars were fought on its eastern frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a>. In the Fourth Frontier War of 1811–1812 British troops drove the Xhosa east of the Fish River and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Grahamstown</a>, which in 1819 survived an attack led by the Xhosa prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda-prophet">Makhanda</a>. The Sixth Frontier War of 1834–1836 led Governor Benjamin D’Urban to annex the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen">Queen Adelaide</a>, an annexation reversed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> in 1836. The War of the Axe of 1846–1847 and the long war of 1850–1853 brought the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keiskamma-river">Keiskamma</a> and Kei rivers. After the Xhosa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-killing of 1856–1857, inspired by the prophecies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nongqawuse">Nongqawuse</a>, famine and resettlement broke Xhosa independence west of the Kei. The Ninth Frontier War of 1877–1878 completed the conquest. The Cape fought the Gun War of 1880–1881 when it tried to disarm the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a>, a costly failure that led it to return Basutoland to direct British rule. Defence relied on British regular troops, colonial units such as the Cape Mounted Riflemen, settler commandos and African auxiliaries. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> of 1899–1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> commandos invaded the Cape, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafeking</a>, and several thousand Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> joined them as rebels.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-charles-somerset"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-charles-somerset.jpg" alt="Lord Charles Somerset" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-charles-somerset">Lord Charles Somerset</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Charles_Henry_Somerset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until the 1820s the governor ruled almost without restraint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-charles-somerset">Lord Charles Somerset</a>, governor from 1814 to 1826, controlled appointments, the press and land grants, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> led to the creation of an advisory council in 1825 and a charter of justice that introduced British-style courts while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-dutch-law">Roman-Dutch law</a>. Freedom of the press was conceded in the late 1820s. In 1853 the Cape received a representative parliament elected on a franchise based on property or income rather than race, so that some Coloured and African men could vote. Responsible government followed in 1872, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-molteno">John Molteno</a> as the first prime minister. The colony expanded steadily: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a> was annexed in 1866, Basutoland was attached from 1871 to 1884, Griqualand West was incorporated in 1880, the Transkeian territories were absorbed between 1879 and 1894, and British Bechuanaland in 1895. Cape politics was shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaner-bond">Afrikaner Bond</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hendrik-hofmeyr-onze-jan">Jan Hofmeyr</a> and by English-speaking mining interests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, prime minister from 1890 to 1896, raised the property qualification for voters through the Franchise and Ballot Act of 1892 and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glen-grey-act">Glen Grey Act</a> of 1894, which created individual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> and local councils in African areas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a>, governor from 1854 to 1861, sought to assimilate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> of British Kaffraria through schools, hospitals and salaried headmen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-town"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-town.jpg" alt="Cape Town" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camps_bay_(53460319478)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Der Berzerker from Washington, DC, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> laid many foundations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. The British occupation, the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> reforms helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a>, in which several thousand Dutch-speaking farmers left the colony from 1835 to found republics in Natal, the Orange Free State and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, the origin of the later conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The frontier wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> set patterns of conquest, land loss and labour migration that shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> for generations. At the same time the Cape’s non-racial qualified franchise and its mission schools, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lovedale-missionary-institution">Lovedale</a>, produced the first generation of educated African leaders, among them the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-tengo-jabavu">John Tengo Jabavu</a>, who influenced the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a> vernacular, and the Afrikaner nationalist movement had early roots in the colony. Diamond mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> pioneered the compound system and the large mining houses, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a>, that later dominated the gold fields of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>. The colony’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-dutch-law">Roman-Dutch law</a>, its parliament and its civil service were carried into the Union of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> became the Union’s legislative capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>’s ambitions, launched from the Cape, drove British expansion north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> into the Rhodesias.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jameson-raid.jpg" alt="Jameson Raid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jameson_Raid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Heysham Overend, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony’s political end came through unification rather than defeat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>’s involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895–1896, an attempt to overthrow the government of the South African Republic, forced his resignation and deepened the divide between the Cape’s English-speaking and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> voters. Under the high commissioner Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-milner">Alfred Milner</a>, tensions with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> of 1899–1902, in which the Cape served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s base and saw rebellion in its northern and western districts. After the British victory, the four colonies of the Cape, Natal, the Transvaal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a> moved towards union to manage railways, customs and the question of African rights in common. Delegates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> of 1908–1909, among them the Cape prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-x-merriman">John X. Merriman</a>, agreed a constitution that kept the Cape’s non-racial franchise within the province but allowed only white men to sit in the Union parliament. On 31 May 1910 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a> of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Its franchise was later dismantled: African voters were removed from the common roll in 1936 and Coloured voters in 1956. The provinces of the Western, Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cape">Northern Cape</a> created in 1994 still cover most of its territory.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1795 and permanently from 1806, turned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> refreshment station into the largest colony in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and its frontier wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a>, abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a>, qualified non-racial franchise and diamond economy shaped the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> formed in 1910.</em></p>

